5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The decisive EJR constraints are the 6-voter ℓ=3 group on {x1,x2,x3} and the 4-voter ℓ=2 group on {y4,y5,y6}. Any valid W must therefore contain at least two x-candidates (so that the first bloc can be satisfied either directly or via voter 1’s private approvals) and at least two seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2}.  

Those two lower bounds leave exactly one auxiliary seat once |W|=5 is reached. That seat can be filled either by a third x-candidate or by y1 (or symmetrically by z3). Both substitutions preserve the y-bloc quota because the remaining y4–y6 or z-candidates still give voters 4–7 two approved members. The resulting committees {x1,x2,y4,y5,y1} and {x1,x2,y4,y5,z3} therefore meet every cohesive-group threshold while giving voter 1 exactly four approvals.  

The symmetric construction with three x-candidates plus one y-seat likewise yields four. At the lower end, two x-candidates plus two y-seats plus z1 yields exactly two approvals for voter 1. One approval is impossible (the ℓ=3 bloc cannot be satisfied) and five is impossible (no room remains for the y-quota). Hence the attainable interval is genuinely [2,4], i.e., choice A. The tighter [2,3] claims appear to rest on an overly rigid reading of how y1 can substitute for one of the demanded y-seats without violating EJR.}

16.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The binding EJR constraints are the 6-voter ℓ=3 group on {x1,x2,x3} and the 4-voter ℓ=2 group on {y4,y5,y6}. Any valid W must therefore contain at least two x-candidates (otherwise no member of the first group reaches three approvals) while also drawing at least two seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2}.  

Those two lower bounds leave exactly one auxiliary seat once |W|=5 is reached. That seat can be assigned to y1 (or symmetrically to z3) without dropping the y-bloc below its entitlement, because y1 is approved by voter 1 alone and does not subtract from the two y4–y6 or z-overlap seats already supplied. Explicit constructions confirm both extremes:  
- W = {x1,x2,y4,y5,z1} meets every cohesive-group quota and intersects voter 1’s ballot in exactly two candidates;  
- W = {x1,x2,y4,y5,y1} likewise meets every quota (the y-bloc still sees two of its approved candidates) yet intersects voter 1’s ballot in exactly four candidates.  

Hence the attainable interval under EJR is genuinely [2,4], i.e., choice A. The stricter upper bound of three rests on an overly rigid reading of the y-quota that the definition itself does not impose once y1 is permitted as a non-conflicting substitute.}

2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The decisive EJR constraints are the 6-voter ℓ=3 group on {x1,x2,x3} and the 4-voter ℓ=2 group on {y4,y5,y6}. The first is met as soon as two x-candidates enter W (with voter 1 personally supplying the third approval via y1 or z3). The second requires only two seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2}.  

These quotas leave three remaining seats that can be filled by two x-candidates plus y1 (or symmetrically z3). The explicit committee W={x1,x2,y4,y5,y1} therefore satisfies both large-coalition lower bounds while giving voter 1 exactly four approvals; the symmetric substitution of z3 for y1 works equally well. Replacing one of the y-seats with y1 never drops any member of the y-bloc below two approved representatives, because the remaining y4–y6 candidates continue to cover the quota.  

Hence every integer from 2 through 4 is attainable and the interval is genuinely [2,4], i.e., choice A. Arguments that force the upper bound down to three rest on an overly rigid reading of the y-quota that the EJR definition itself does not impose.}

